  "title": "Mama Mboga's Kibanda",
  "category": "food-drink",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 5,
  "order": 501,
  "theme": "market",
  "culturalNote": "Every Nairobi estate has a mama mboga — the \"vegetable lady\" with a wooden stall (kibanda) by the road, selling tomatoes, onions and sukuma wiki in small bundles. Prices are quoted per heap (\"tatu kwa mia\" = three for a hundred) rather than per kilo, and almost everyone pays with M-Pesa. Nouns like nyanya (tomato) are n/n: the same word for one or many, so \"this tomato\" is nyanya hii and \"these tomatoes\" is nyanya hizi.",
  "startingPoint": "It's evening. You stop at the mama mboga's stall on your way home from work.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "negative-present",
    "na-have",
    "demonstratives",
    "numbers-money"
  ],
